# Creating an Entity in SAP Cloud Application Programming Model (CAP)

In the SAP Cloud Application Programming Model (CAP), entities are defined in the data model using CDS (Core Data Services) syntax. Here's how to create an entity:

## Basic Entity Definition

```cds
namespace my.namespace;

entity Products {
    key ID    : Integer;
    name      : String(100);
    price     : Decimal(9,2);
    currency  : String(3) default 'USD';
    stock     : Integer default 0;
}
```

## Key Aspects

### 1. Namespace
- Declare a namespace to organize your entities
- Example: `namespace my.namespace;`

### 2. Entity Structure
- Use the `entity` keyword followed by the entity name
- Define fields with their data types
- Specify a `key` field (required)

### 3. Common Data Types
- `String(length)`: Text field with optional length
- `Integer`: Whole numbers
- `Decimal(precision,scale)`: Decimal numbers
- `Boolean`: True/false values
- `Date`, `DateTime`, `Timestamp`: Date and time types

## Advanced Features

### Associations (Relationships)
```cds
entity Orders {
    key ID : Integer;
    items  : Composition of many OrderItems on items.order = $self;
    customer : Association to Customers;
}

entity OrderItems {
    key ID : Integer;
    order : Association to Orders;
    product : Association to Products;
    quantity : Integer;
}
```

### Annotations
```cds
entity Products @(
    title: 'Product Catalog',
    description: 'Master data for products'
) {
    key ID @title: 'Product ID';
    name @title: 'Product Name' @assert.format: '^[A-Z].*';
    // ...
}
```

### Extending Existing Entities
```cds
extend Products with {
    description : String(1000);
    category    : Association to Categories;
}
```

## Best Practices

1. Always define a key field
2. Use meaningful names for entities and fields
3. Consider adding descriptions with `@title` annotations
4. Use associations instead of foreign keys for relationships
5. Group related entities in the same namespace

After defining your entities in a `.cds` file, you can:
- Deploy them to a database
- Expose them as OData services
- Use them in service implementations

