# Rust Program to Filter and Download YouTube URLs

Here's a Rust program that reads URLs from a file, separates YouTube URLs from others, and downloads the YouTube videos using `yt-dlp`:

```rust
use std::fs::File;
use std::io::{BufRead, BufReader, Write};
use std::process::Command;
use std::path::Path;

fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    // Input and output file paths
    let input_file = "urls.txt";
    let youtube_output_file = "youtube_urls.txt";
    let other_output_file = "other_urls.txt";

    // Check if yt-dlp is installed
    if !is_ytdlp_installed() {
        return Err("yt-dlp is not installed. Please install it first.".into());
    }

    // Open input file
    let file = File::open(input_file)?;
    let reader = BufReader::new(file);

    // Create output files
    let mut youtube_file = File::create(youtube_output_file)?;
    let mut other_file = File::create(other_output_file)?;

    println!("Processing URLs from {}...", input_file);

    for line in reader.lines() {
        let url = line?;

        if is_youtube_url(&url) {
            // Write to YouTube file
            writeln!(youtube_file, "{}", url)?;

            // Download the video
            println!("Downloading YouTube video: {}", url);
            download_youtube_video(&url)?;
        } else {
            // Write to other URLs file
            writeln!(other_file, "{}", url)?;
        }
    }

    println!("Processing complete.");
    println!("YouTube URLs saved to: {}", youtube_output_file);
    println!("Other URLs saved to: {}", other_output_file);

    Ok(())
}

fn is_youtube_url(url: &str) -> bool {
    url.contains("youtube.com") || url.contains("youtu.be")
}

fn is_ytdlp_installed() -> bool {
    Command::new("yt-dlp")
        .arg("--version")
        .output()
        .map(|output| output.status.success())
        .unwrap_or(false)
}

fn download_youtube_video(url: &str) -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    // Create a downloads directory if it doesn't exist
    let downloads_dir = "downloads";
    if !Path::new(downloads_dir).exists() {
        std::fs::create_dir(downloads_dir)?;
    }

    // Execute yt-dlp command
    let status = Command::new("yt-dlp")
        .arg("-o")
        .arg(format!("{}/%(title)s.%(ext)s", downloads_dir))
        .arg(url)
        .status()?;

    if !status.success() {
        return Err(format!("Failed to download video: {}", url).into());
    }

    Ok(())
}
```

## How to Use This Program

1. **Prerequisites**:
   - Install Rust (https://www.rust-lang.org/tools/install)
   - Install `yt-dlp` (https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp#installation)

2. **Create an input file**:
   - Create a file named `urls.txt` in the same directory as the program
   - Add URLs to this file, one per line (both YouTube and non-YouTube URLs)

3. **Run the program**:
   ```sh
   cargo run
   ```

## What the Program Does

1. Reads URLs from `urls.txt`
2. Separates them into:
   - YouTube URLs (saved to `youtube_urls.txt`)
   - Other URLs (saved to `other_urls.txt`)
3. Downloads each YouTube video using `yt-dlp` and saves them in a `downloads` directory
4. Provides feedback about the process in the console

## Customization Options

You can modify the `download_youtube_video` function to change download options. For example:

- Add `--format` to specify video quality
- Add `--extract-audio` to download only audio
- Add `--write-thumbnail` to download thumbnails

Example modification:
```rust
Command::new("yt-dlp")
    .arg("-o")
    .arg(format!("{}/%(title)s.%(ext)s", downloads_dir))
    .arg("--format")
    .arg("bestvideo[height<=1080]+bestaudio/best[height<=1080]")
    .arg(url)
    .status()?;
```

# Sierpinski Triangle in XAML

Here's a XAML implementation of a Sierpinski triangle using a recursive approach with Path elements:

```xml
<Window x:Class="SierpinskiTriangle.MainWindow"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        Title="Sierpinski Triangle" Height="500" Width="500">
    <Canvas Name="mainCanvas" Background="White">
        <!-- Initial triangle points -->
        <Path Fill="Black">
            <Path.Data>
                <PathGeometry>
                    <PathFigure StartPoint="250,50" IsClosed="True">
                        <LineSegment Point="50,450"/>
                        <LineSegment Point="450,450"/>
                    </PathFigure>
                </PathGeometry>
            </Path.Data>
        </Path>

        <!-- First recursive level -->
        <Path Fill="White">
            <Path.Data>
                <PathGeometry>
                    <PathFigure StartPoint="250,150" IsClosed="True">
                        <LineSegment Point="100,350"/>
                        <LineSegment Point="400,350"/>
                    </PathFigure>
                </PathGeometry>
            </Path.Data>
        </Path>

        <!-- Second recursive level -->
        <Path Fill="Black">
            <Path.Data>
                <PathGeometry>
                    <PathFigure StartPoint="250,200" IsClosed="True">
                        <LineSegment Point="175,300"/>
                        <LineSegment Point="325,300"/>
                    </PathFigure>
                </PathGeometry>
            </Path.Data>
        </Path>
    </Canvas>
</Window>
```

For a more complete implementation with multiple levels, you would typically use code-behind to generate the triangles recursively. Here's a C# version that creates a proper recursive Sierpinski triangle:

```csharp
using System.Windows;
using System.Windows.Media;
using System.Windows.Shapes;

public partial class MainWindow : Window
{
    public MainWindow()
    {
        InitializeComponent();
        DrawSierpinski(mainCanvas, new Point(250, 50), new Point(50, 450), new Point(450, 450), 5);
    }

    private void DrawSierpinski(Canvas canvas, Point a, Point b, Point c, int depth)
    {
        if (depth == 0)
        {
            var triangle = new Path
            {
                Fill = Brushes.Black,
                Data = new PathGeometry
                {
                    Figures = new PathFigureCollection
                    {
                        new PathFigure
                        {
                            StartPoint = a,
                            IsClosed = true,
                            Segments = new PathSegmentCollection
                            {
                                new LineSegment(b, true),
                                new LineSegment(c, true)
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            };
            canvas.Children.Add(triangle);
        }
        else
        {
            var ab = new Point((a.X + b.X) / 2, (a.Y + b.Y) / 2);
            var ac = new Point((a.X + c.X) / 2, (a.Y + c.Y) / 2);
            var bc = new Point((b.X + c.X) / 2, (b.Y + c.Y) / 2);

            DrawSierpinski(canvas, a, ab, ac, depth - 1);
            DrawSierpinski(canvas, ab, b, bc, depth - 1);
            DrawSierpinski(canvas, ac, bc, c, depth - 1);
        }
    }
}
```

The XAML-only version shows a basic approximation with three levels, while the code-behind version creates a true recursive Sierpinski triangle with the depth you specify (5 in this example). The recursive approach divides the triangle into smaller triangles at each level.

# C# Program with POST Request and Client Certificate

Here's a complete C# program that sends a POST request with a client certificate attached:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Net;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

class Program
{
    static async Task Main(string[] args)
    {
        // Configuration
        string url = "https://example.com/api/endpoint"; // Replace with your target URL
        string jsonContent = "{\"key\":\"value\"}"; // Replace with your JSON payload
        string certificatePath = @"C:\path\to\your\certificate.pfx"; // Replace with your certificate path
        string certificatePassword = "yourPassword"; // Replace with your certificate password

        try
        {
            // Load the client certificate
            var certificate = new X509Certificate2(
                fileName: certificatePath,
                password: certificatePassword,
                keyStorageFlags: X509KeyStorageFlags.MachineKeySet | 
                                X509KeyStorageFlags.PersistKeySet | 
                                X509KeyStorageFlags.Exportable);

            // Create HttpClientHandler with the certificate
            var handler = new HttpClientHandler();
            handler.ClientCertificateOptions = ClientCertificateOption.Manual;
            handler.SslProtocols = System.Security.Authentication.SslProtocols.Tls12;
            handler.ClientCertificates.Add(certificate);

            // Optionally bypass certificate validation (for testing only)
            // handler.ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback = (sender, cert, chain, sslPolicyErrors) => true;

            // Create HttpClient with the handler
            using (var client = new HttpClient(handler))
            {
                // Set timeout
                client.Timeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30);

                // Create request content
                var httpContent = new StringContent(jsonContent, System.Text.Encoding.UTF8, "application/json");

                // Send POST request
                Console.WriteLine($"Sending POST request to {url}");
                HttpResponseMessage response = await client.PostAsync(url, httpContent);

                // Process response
                Console.WriteLine($"Response status: {response.StatusCode}");
                string responseBody = await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
                Console.WriteLine($"Response body: {responseBody}");
            }
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Error: {ex.Message}");
            if (ex.InnerException != null)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"Inner exception: {ex.InnerException.Message}");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

## Important Notes:

1. **Certificate Configuration**:
   - The certificate should be in PFX format (PKCS#12) containing both public and private keys
   - Make sure the certificate password is correct
   - Adjust the `X509KeyStorageFlags` as needed for your environment

2. **Security**:
   - The commented-out `ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback` bypasses SSL validation - only use this for testing
   - In production, properly validate server certificates

3. **Error Handling**:
   - The code includes basic error handling, but you might want to expand it for your specific needs

4. **TLS Version**:
   - The code explicitly uses TLS 1.2. Adjust if you need a different version

5. **NuGet Package**:
   - This uses `HttpClient` which is available in .NET Core/.NET 5+. For older .NET Framework versions, you might need to adjust the implementation.

