# Extracting Hashtags from Text in C#

Here's how you can extract hashtags from a string in C#:

## Method 1: Using Regular Expressions (Regex)

```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class HashtagExtractor
{
    public static List<string> ExtractHashtags(string text)
    {
        var hashtags = new List<string>();
        var regex = new Regex(@"(?<=#)\w+", RegexOptions.Compiled);

        foreach (Match match in regex.Matches(text))
        {
            if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(match.Value))
            {
                hashtags.Add(match.Value);
            }
        }

        return hashtags;
    }

    // Alternative version that includes the '#' in results
    public static List<string> ExtractHashtagsWithHash(string text)
    {
        var hashtags = new List<string>();
        var regex = new Regex(@"(#\w+)", RegexOptions.Compiled);

        foreach (Match match in regex.Matches(text))
        {
            if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(match.Value))
            {
                hashtags.Add(match.Value);
            }
        }

        return hashtags;
    }
}
```

### Usage Example:

```csharp
string text = "This is a #test string with multiple #hashtags like #CSharp and #Programming.";
var hashtags = HashtagExtractor.ExtractHashtags(text);

foreach (var tag in hashtags)
{
    Console.WriteLine(tag);
}
```

## Method 2: Using String Operations (for simple cases)

```csharp
public static List<string> ExtractHashtagsSimple(string text)
{
    var hashtags = new List<string>();
    int startIndex = -1;

    for (int i = 0; i < text.Length; i++)
    {
        if (text[i] == '#')
        {
            startIndex = i;
        }
        else if (startIndex != -1 && char.IsWhiteSpace(text[i]))
        {
            if (i - startIndex > 1) // At least one character after #
            {
                hashtags.Add(text.Substring(startIndex, i - startIndex));
            }
            startIndex = -1;
        }
    }

    // Add the last hashtag if text ends with one
    if (startIndex != -1 && startIndex < text.Length - 1)
    {
        hashtags.Add(text.Substring(startIndex));
    }

    return hashtags;
}
```

## Notes:

1. The regex pattern `#\w+` matches:
   - `#` - the hashtag symbol
   - `\w+` - one or more word characters (letters, digits, underscore)

2. If you need more complex hashtag matching (like supporting non-English characters), you might need to adjust the regex pattern.

3. The first regex method returns just the text after the #, while the second includes the # symbol in the results.

4. These methods will find all hashtags in the text, including those in the middle of words (like "test#hashtag") - you may want to add additional checks if this isn't desired behavior.

